];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Omvia - Smart Home UX/UI Case Study"
  description="Omvia is a human-centric smart home ecosystem designed to reduce app fatigue through a soft-tech interface, responsive prototype, and caregiver-friendly wellness checks."
>
  <Header />

  <main class="project-detail">
    <section class="project-hero site-shell section">
      <a href="/#work" class="project-back-link">← Work</a>

      <div class="project-hero__intro">
        <p class="project-number">{project.number}</p>

        <div>
          <p class="project-label">{project.category} - 2025</p>
          <h1>{project.title}</h1>

          <p class="project-hero__summary">
            Omvia brings smart-home routines, devices, safety, and caregiving into one calm, clear interface, making everyday home management easier without jumping between multiple apps.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="project-hero__image">
        <img src="/images/projects/omvia/hero.png" alt="Omvia app hero mockup" />
      </figure>

      <dl class="project-facts">
        <div>
          <dt>Role</dt>
          <dd>Product Designer, 3-person team</dd>
        </div>

       <div>
          <dt>Contribution</dt>
          <dd>
            Led UI design and visual direction; contributed to research, user flows,
            branding, prototyping, and testing.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt>Tools</dt>
          <dd>Figma, FigJam, Basecamp, Maze</dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt>Timeframe</dt>
          <dd>Sep 2025 - Jan 2026</dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt>Year</dt>
          <dd>2025</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <section class="omvia-test-results" aria-labelledby="omvia-test-results-title">
        <h2 id="omvia-test-results-title">USABILITY TEST RESULTS</h2>
        <dl class="omvia-test-results__metrics">
          <div>
            <dt>Participants</dt>
            <dd>20</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Remote safety task</dt>
            <dd>65% → 92%</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Caregiver task</dt>
            <dd>80% → 100%</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Routine task</dt>
            <dd>70% → 100%</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section site-shell">
      <div class="project-section__label">
        <p>01 / The Problem</p>
      </div>

      <div class="project-section__content">
        <p class="project-lead">
          Smart home technology should make daily life easier, but managing multiple apps, devices, routines, and alerts can quickly become another source of stress.
        </p>

        <div class="number-list">
          {problemPoints.map((item, index) => (
            <article>
              <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
              <div>
                <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                <p>{item.text}</p>
              </div>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="question-block goal-block">
          <h3>The goal</h3>
          <p>
            Create one calm, easy-to-use smart home experience that supports everyday routines, remote safety, and caregiving without adding more complexity.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section site-shell omvia-research">
      <div class="project-section__label">
        <p>02 / Research</p>
      </div>

      <div class="project-section__content">
        <p class="project-lead">
          Research began with a review of existing smart-home products and early problem-framing exercises. The goal was to narrow the broad smart-home space into specific situations where a more focused experience could be useful.
        </p>

        <div class="question-block">
          <h3>Looking at Existing Products</h3>
          <p>Google Home and Samsung SmartThings were reviewed to understand how established products organize devices, routines, alerts, and home controls.</p>
          <div class="split-copy">
          {competitorAnalysis.map((item) => (
            <article>
              <img src={item.logo} alt={item.logoAlt} />
              <h3>{item.product}</h3>
              <p>
                <span class="inline-label">Strength:</span>{" "}
                {item.strength}
              </p>
              <p>
                <span class="inline-label">Opportunity:</span>{" "}
                {item.opportunity}
              </p>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
          <div class="omvia-research__takeaway">
            <h4>Key Takeaway</h4>
            <p>Both products offered strong device control, but managing a connected home could still involve moving between devices, rooms, settings, and alerts. This created an opportunity to make everyday home management feel more focused and easier to understand.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-block">
          <h3>Framing the Problem</h3>
          <p>A problem-framing exercise explored who might experience friction, when it could happen, and which situations were worth examining more closely.</p>
          <figure class="omvia-research__board omvia-research__board--main">
            <button type="button" class="omvia-board-open" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-label="Enlarge brainstorming board"><img src="/images/projects/omvia/omvia-problem-framing.png" alt="Omvia problem-framing brainstorming board exploring who, what, when, where, and why around smart-home management" loading="lazy" /></button>
            <figcaption>Early exploration considered busy households, homeowners managing a property while away, and adults supporting older family members.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="question-block">
          <h3>Exploring User Needs</h3>
          <p>Three situations were explored further to understand the different needs that could shape the experience.</p>
          <div class="omvia-research__boards">
            <figure class="omvia-research__board">
              <button type="button" class="omvia-board-open" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-label="Enlarge brainstorming board"><img src="/images/projects/omvia/omvia-user-need-family.png" alt="Omvia brainstorming board exploring smart-home needs for busy families with children" loading="lazy" /></button>
              <figcaption>Family with Kids</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="omvia-research__board">
              <button type="button" class="omvia-board-open" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-label="Enlarge brainstorming board"><img src="/images/projects/omvia/omvia-user-need-away.png" alt="Omvia brainstorming board exploring remote smart-home needs for homeowners away from home" loading="lazy" /></button>
              <figcaption>Away from Home</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="omvia-research__board">
              <button type="button" class="omvia-board-open" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-label="Enlarge brainstorming board"><img src="/images/projects/omvia/omvia-user-need-caregiver.png" alt="Omvia brainstorming board exploring smart-home needs for adults supporting an older parent" loading="lazy" /></button>
              <figcaption>Supporting an Older Parent</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-block">
          <h3>Narrowing the Focus</h3>
          <div class="omvia-research__focus">
            <article>
              <h4>Everyday routines</h4>
              <p>Busy households may need a quicker way to manage repeated actions across several devices.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h4>Away from home</h4>
              <p>Homeowners may need an easier way to check important home information and control devices remotely.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h4>Supporting family</h4>
              <p>Family members may need useful updates and remote support without constantly monitoring someone.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="project-section site-shell">
      <div class="project-section__label">
        <p>03 / From Needs to Solutions</p>
      </div>

      <div class="project-section__content">
        <p class="project-lead">
          The three focus areas were translated into product decisions that shaped Omvia’s core experience.
        </p>

        <div class="solution-pathways">
          <div class="solution-pathways__header">
            <span>User need</span>
            <span>Pain point</span>
            <span>Omvia response</span>
          </div>

          {userSolutions.map((item, index) => (
            <article class="solution-pathway">
              <div class="solution-pathway__persona">
                <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
                <div>
                  <h3>{item.user}</h3>
                  <p>{item.role}</p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="solution-pathway__problem">
                <p>{item.problem}</p>
              </div>

              <div class="solution-pathway__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>

              <div class="solution-pathway__solution">
                <span>{item.feature}</span>
                <h3>{item.solution}</h3>
                <p>{item.note}</p>
              </div>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section site-shell">
      <div class="project-section__label">
        <p>04 / Structure</p>
      </div>

      <div class="project-section__content">
        <p class="project-lead">
          The app was organized around five core areas: Home, Devices, Routines, Alerts, and Settings, keeping the main experience clear and easy to navigate.
        </p>

        <figure class="project-figure">
          <img src="/images/projects/omvia/sitemap.png" alt="Omvia sitemap" />
        </figure>

      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section site-shell">
  <div class="project-section__label">
    <p>05 / Early Design</p>
  </div>

  <div class="project-section__content">
    <p class="project-lead">
      Low-fidelity wireframes were used to work through the dashboard hierarchy, five-part navigation, and routine setup flow before adding the final visual system.
    </p>

    <div class="lofi-showcase">
      <div class="lofi-board-grid">
        <article class="lofi-screen-card lofi-screen-card--onboarding">
          <div class="lofi-screen-card__image">
            <img
              src="/images/projects/omvia/lofi-onboarding.png"
              alt="Omvia low-fidelity onboarding wireframe"
            />
          </div>
          <p>Onboarding</p>
        </article>

       <article class="lofi-screen-card lofi-screen-card--home">
  <div class="lofi-screen-card__image">
    <img
      src="/images/projects/omvia/lofi-home-dashboard.png"
      alt="Omvia low-fidelity home dashboard wireframe"
    />
    <span class="lofi-marker lofi-marker--one">01</span>
    <span class="lofi-marker lofi-marker--two">02</span>
  </div>
  <p>Home Dashboard</p>
</article>

        <article class="lofi-screen-card lofi-screen-card--devices">
          <div class="lofi-screen-card__image">
            <img
              src="/images/projects/omvia/lofi-devices.png"
              alt="Omvia low-fidelity devices wireframe"
            />
            
          </div>
          <p>Devices</p>
        </article>

        <article class="lofi-screen-card lofi-screen-card--routine">
          <div class="lofi-screen-card__image">
            <img
              src="/images/projects/omvia/lofi-routine-setup.png"
              alt="Omvia low-fidelity routine setup wireframe"
            />
            <span class="lofi-marker lofi-marker--three">03</span>
          </div>
          <p>Routine Setup</p>
        </article>

        <article class="lofi-screen-card lofi-screen-card--alert">
          <div class="lofi-screen-card__image">
            <img
              src="/images/projects/omvia/lofi-alert.png"
              alt="Omvia low-fidelity alert wireframe"
            />
          </div>
          <p>Alert</p>
        </article>

        <article class="lofi-screen-card lofi-screen-card--settings">
  <div class="lofi-screen-card__image">
    <img
      src="/images/projects/omvia/lofi-settings.png"
      alt="Omvia low-fidelity settings wireframe"
    />
  </div>
  <p>Settings</p>
</article>
      </div>

      <div class="lofi-showcase__caption">
        <p>What this stage helped define</p>
      </div>

      <div class="lofi-showcase__notes">
        <article>
          <span>01</span>
          <h3>Dashboard hierarchy</h3>
          <p>
    Worked out how home status, routines, and important updates should be prioritized on the main dashboard.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <span>02</span>
          <h3>Five-part navigation</h3>
          <p>
            Established Home, Devices, Routines, Alerts, and Settings as the main areas of the app.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <span>03</span>
          <h3>Routine setup flow</h3>
          <p>
            Defined the steps for moving from routine selection and setup to activation.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

    <section class="project-section site-shell">
      <div class="project-section__label">
        <p>06 / Visual Direction</p>
      </div>

      <div class="project-section__content">
        <p class="project-lead">
          The visual direction shaped Omvia into a softer smart-home experience: calm instead of clinical, familiar instead of technical, and reassuring at a glance.
        </p>

        <div class="design-principles">
          {designPrinciples.map((item) => (
            <article>
              <h3>{item.title}</h3>
              <p>{item.text}</p>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h3 class="subheading visual-direction__subheading">Logo exploration</h3>

        <div class="logo-grid logo-grid--selection">
          <figure class="logo-option">
            <img src="/images/projects/omvia/logo-v1.png" alt="Omvia logo version one, The Routine Loop" />
            <figcaption>Version 1 - The Routine Loop</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <figure class="logo-option logo-option--chosen">
            <span class="logo-option__badge">Chosen direction</span>
            <img src="/images/projects/omvia/logo-v2.png" alt="Omvia chosen logo, The Home Pulse" />
            <figcaption>Version 2 - The Home Pulse</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="logo-decision">
          <p class="logo-decision__label">Why The Home Pulse</p>

          <p>
            The Home Pulse was chosen because the house shape made the smart-home purpose immediately clear, while the pulse line connected to safety, routines, and Wellness Checks. The Routine Loop communicated connection, but felt more abstract.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="subheading">Colour Palette</h3>

        <div class="palette-grid">
          {palette.map((color) => (
            <article>
              <span style={`--swatch: ${color.value};`}></span>

              <div>
                <h3>{color.name}</h3>
                <p>{color.value}</p>
                <small>{color.text}</small>
              </div>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h3 class="subheading">UI System</h3>

        <p class="project-copy">
          Reusable components were created to keep controls, states, and room-based device management consistent across Omvia.
        </p>

        <figure class="project-figure">
          <img
            src="/images/projects/omvia/ui-system.png"
            alt="Omvia reusable UI components including text fields, checkboxes, toggles, buttons, room selectors, room controls, and device cards"
          />
        </figure>

        <h3 class="subheading">Moodboard</h3>

        <p class="project-copy">
          Visual research focused on interfaces and imagery that felt calm, approachable, and trustworthy rather than highly technical. References were collected around soft colour, rounded forms, clear hierarchy, and subtle status cues to shape Omvia’s soft-tech direction.
        </p>

        <figure class="project-figure">
          <img src="/images/projects/omvia/moodboard.png" alt="Omvia visual system moodboard" />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section site-shell no-reveal">
      <div class="project-section__label">
        <p>07 / Final Experience</p>
      </div>

      <div class="project-section__content">
        <p class="project-lead">
          The final interface brings the core flows together across onboarding, routines, devices, settings, and alerts, with more space for the product itself to take focus.
        </p>

        {hifiSections.map((section) => (
          <div class="flow-section">
            <h3 class="subheading">{section.title}</h3>

            <p class="project-copy">
              {section.intro}
            </p>

            <div class:list={["screen-flow", `screen-flow--${section.variant}`]}>
              <div class="screen-flow__backdrop"></div>

